{
 "title": "Mining Financial Data using Mixtures of Mirrored Weibull Distributions - Replication Spec",
 "hypothesis": "The mixture of mirrored Weibull (MMW) distribution provides more accurate and reliable VaR estimates/forecasts than Gaussian mixture (GMM) and t-mixture (tMM) for S&P500 returns.",
 "data_sources": [
  "Adjusted closing prices for S&P500 stocks Berkshire Hathaway (BRK), Walmart (WMT), CVS Health Corporation (CVS) downloaded for period June 1996 - May 2024 (monthly, BRK/WMT) and July 2019 - Jun 2024 (daily, CVS) and converted to log price ratio returns"
 ],
 "sample": "Three S&P500 stocks: BRK monthly returns, WMT monthly returns, CVS daily returns. Returns calculated on adjusted closing prices as log price ratio. Table 1 caption states period mid-1997 (*mid-2009) to 2024; text states June 1996 to May 2024 (BRK/WMT) and July 2019 to Jun 2024 (CVS). No filter beyond that stated. Sample sizes n per stock not printed.",
 "period_start": "June 1996 (Table says mid-1997; text says June 1996 for BRK/WMT; July 2019 for CVS)",
 "period_end": "May 2024 (monthly BRK/WMT); Jun 2024 (daily CVS) (Table says 2024)",
 "method": [
  "1. Compute log returns x_j = log(P_t / P_{t-1}) on adjusted close, monthly for BRK/WMT, daily for CVS",
  "2. Mirror transformation: y_j = c - x_j where c = ceil(|x_(n)|) with x_(n) = largest observation in sample. Mirrored Weibull density (2) defined for x <= c",
  "3. MMW density (3): f(x;Psi)=sum_{i=1}^g pi_i f_MW(x;mu_i,sigma_i), g components, Psi = (pi_1..pi_{g-1},mu_1..mu_g,sigma_1..sigma_g), m=3g-1 free parameters",
  "4. Fit by EM maximum likelihood. Log-likelihood (4). Initialization: initial clustering via k-means/hierarchical/random -> z_ij^(0), pi_i^(0)=sum_j z_ij^(0), sigma_i^(0) solves s_i^2/bar{y}_i^2 = [Gamma(1+2/sigma_i)-Gamma(1+1/sigma_i)^2]/Gamma(1+1/sigma_i)^2 numerically, mu_i^(0)=bar{y}_i[Gamma(1+1/sigma_i^(0))]^{-1}",
  "5. E-step: z_ij^(k)= pi_i^(k) f_W(y_j;mu_i^(k),sigma_i^(k)) / sum_h pi_h^(k) f_W(y_j;mu_h^(k),sigma_h^(k))",
  "6. M-step: pi_i^(k)=1/n sum_j z_ij^(k) (5); mu_i^(k) solves (6) numerically [modified approximation of b20 allowed]; sigma_i^(k)=[sum_j z_ij^(k) y_j^{mu_i^(k)}/sum_j z_ij^(k)]^{1/mu_i^(k)} (7)",
  "7. Stopping: terminate if k >= k_max=500 or |l^(k)-l^(k-1)| < epsilon=1e-6 with l^(k) as defined",
  "8. Choose g: run EM for g=1,2,3,4, select lowest BIC = m log n -2 log hat{l}. Most frequent chosen g=2",
  "9. Fit GMM and tMM analogously for comparison",
  "10. VaR definition: VaR_alpha = F_X^{-1}(1-alpha), cdf of X = loss distribution. Focus alpha=1% and 5%",
  "11. VaR estimation: quantile of fitted mixture via closed-form cdf or simulation approximation. In-sample trained using full 7 years of historical monthly log returns (as printed for BRK)",
  "12. One-day VaR forecast (CVS): train on previous 250 days (~1 year) rolling window, forecast next day VaR, advance window 1 day to last available date, compare to historical method",
  "13. Assessment: Kupiec proportion-of-failures (unconditional coverage) test LR_POF and Christoffersen independence test LR_IND reported with p-values"
 ],
